```json
{
  "items": [
    { "id": "post_123", "title": "...", "thumbnailUrl": "..." }
  ],
  "nextCursor": "eyJpZCI6InBvc3RfMTIzIiwidHMiOjE3MjAwMDAwMDB9",
  "hasMore": true
}
```

The `nextCursor` is an opaque token — the client doesn't parse or construct it, just passes it back verbatim on the next request. That opacity is deliberate: it lets the server change the cursor's internal encoding (switching what it's actually based on) without breaking clients, since the client never depended on its internal structure. `hasMore` lets the client stop requesting further pages without needing to infer it from an empty `items` array, which is a real edge case (a page landing exactly on the last item) that's easy to get subtly wrong without an explicit flag.
